{"kind":"artifact","text":".github/dependabot.yml at exact clean pushed head 49bc0c9788560ac31247945df0807cd03e0fa8de, fulfilling c488f287 under promise ce7c1752. Dependabot covers the Go module at the root, npm under ui, and GitHub Actions, on a weekly cadence with a bounded open-PR limit. The gate pins the directory for each ecosystem, so pointing npm at the wrong directory fails rather than silently watching nothing.\n\nGATES I RAN AT THIS EXACT HEAD: gofmt clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... green across every package; go mod verify all modules verified; the secret-scanner's own test suite, all 13 cases; GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build ./... clean; git diff --check clean; worktree clean; origin equals the head.\n\nFIVE MUTATIONS I RAN MYSELF, and a warning about two of them. Disabling the workflow-level permissions check kills six negatives, each naming the diagnostic it expected. Disabling the job-override check kills the three job-override negatives. Disabling the container refusal kills job_container_image. Disabling the commit-pin requirement kills the unpinned and short-SHA negatives. Changing ci.yml's make ui-check to make ui kills TestCIVerifiesTheCommittedUIEmbed. Every one fails because the intended diagnostic is absent, not merely because something failed -- these tests pin the reason, so a guard that refused for the wrong reason would still be caught.\n\nTWO OF MY FIRST ATTEMPTS DID NOT APPLY AND LOOKED LIKE SURVIVORS. My first permissions mutation and my first container mutation were written against the wrong indentation and the wrong field name, so the file was unchanged and the suite passed. Nothing survived; my edits missed. I checked which lines had actually changed before believing either result, redid both against the real lines, and both then killed their tests. A mutation that does not apply is indistinguishable from a mutation that survives unless you look at the diff.","body":{"commit":"49bc0c9788560ac31247945df0807cd03e0fa8de","path":".github/dependabot.yml"}}